h each other in some way  shape or form over the course of time so  i think that's why forgiveness is at the  front of my mind  i love that that's a great addition  thank you so much  eddie i wonder we some of us i know you  and me we're in  lockdown and and many people who are  listening to this call might be in  lockdown  i wonder is there a time in your life  when you've thought most deeply about  kindness there's been a few different  occasions i think throughout my life  where kindness  as as a concept as something which is  meaningful to me  personally has come into my life uh  probably the one that  is really the strongest memory that i  can think of  is the time when i was in early primary  school  i didn't have a fun time as a young boy  at school i have an older brother and an  older sister and  my older sister who's five years older  than me um there was a very small  overlap where i just started  you know kindergarten in year one and  she was up there in in the big leagues  with the other big kids  but shortly after that when she left i  remember being quite  lonely in primary school being quite  young  not feeling very much alike the people  around me  and so that was a time when i i think i  was thinking about kindness because  my experience often with my peers was  not  kindness i don't make friends easily i  know this is a bit surprising to some  people but i'm actually a classic  introvert  so it takes me a long time to warm up to  people personally  uh and and to trust them and certainly  if you went back to  seven or eight year old eddie he was not  very good  at making friends um and was sort of  desperate  to receive kindness from people and and  didn't know how to do that so  i think that that's a period of my life  the last many years that's  uh had a deep impact on the kind of  person i am now  and especially the kind of teacher i am  now because young people  um they are my work and the thing that i  love going to school for  every day i wan